\chapter{Files, Blocks, and the Problem of Tomorrow}
\label{ch:files}
\index{filesystems}\index{inodes}\index{buffer cache}\index{persistence}

\section{A name is a route to an object}

A pathname is not the file itself. It is a route through directories to
an object. A directory associates names with filesystem identifiers;
an inode records metadata and information about where a file's contents
can be found. The precise on-disk layout varies, but separating names
from objects is a central Unix idea.

A hard link adds another directory entry referring to the same object.
A symbolic link stores a path to be interpreted during lookup.
Their behavior differs when targets move, disappear, or cross filesystem
boundaries. A hard link ordinarily cannot cross the boundary between
filesystems because the identifier it stores belongs to one filesystem's
namespace. A symbolic link can name a path that resolves elsewhere.

Permission checking also follows the route. Finding a file requires the
appropriate search permission on directories along the way, not merely
read permission on the final object. Reading a directory's list of names
and searching it for a known name are distinct operations. This is one
reason a simple ``read/write/execute'' summary can mislead when applied
to directories \parencite[ch. 4]{apue}.

\section{Three levels of an open file}
\index{file descriptors}\index{open file descriptions}

A descriptor is an integer in a process's descriptor table.
It refers to kernel state for an open file description, which in turn
refers to an underlying object. The description contains properties such
as the current offset and relevant status flags. Multiple descriptors
can share it through duplication or inheritance.

If a parent forks, an inherited descriptor can refer to the same open
file description in parent and child. Their reads can consequently affect
a shared offset. If they independently open the same pathname, they
normally acquire separate descriptions with separate offsets.
The underlying file can be identical in both cases; the opening state is
what differs.

A compact diagram captures the distinction:

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={draw,font=\small,align=center}]
\node (a) at (0,0.7) {Parent fd};
\node (b) at (0,-0.7) {Child fd};
\node (o) at (4,0) {Open description\\offset, flags};
\node (i) at (8,0) {Filesystem object\\metadata, blocks};
\draw[->] (a) -- (o);
\draw[->] (b) -- (o);
\draw[->] (o) -- (i);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

Lions traces the corresponding file and inode tables in Sixth Edition.
Modern terminology and structures vary, but the lifetime distinction
remains useful. It explains why closing one descriptor need not close
all access to the object, and why descriptor leaks are resource leaks
rather than merely unused integers \parencite[ch. 18]{lions}.

Unlinking removes a name. A file can continue to exist while an open
reference remains after its last directory link is removed.
Reclamation follows the relevant link and reference lifetimes.
This makes temporary-file patterns possible, but secure temporary-file
creation requires more than inventing a name from a process ID.
The historical practice described by Lions should not be copied as modern
security guidance.

\section{Bytes become blocks}

The file interface lets a program read a range of bytes.
The storage device transfers blocks or sectors under different constraints.
The kernel maps logical file positions to storage locations and keeps
partial transfers and metadata out of the application's ordinary logic.
The abstraction is convenient because applications need not know the
disk's physical geometry.

Sixth Edition uses its own inode addressing scheme, including the
historical distinction between small and large files. Thompson's later
implementation paper describes another layout with ten direct addresses
and indirect levels. These are different snapshots. The commonly taught
modern diagram of direct, single-indirect, double-indirect, and
triple-indirect pointers must not be pasted over every historical inode
\parencite[ch. 19]{lions}\parencite[sec. 4]{implementation}.

Indirect addressing trades metadata work against maximum size and
compactness for small files. A small file can avoid traversing several
levels of mapping. A large file can acquire more blocks without requiring
an enormous fixed-size inode. Modern extent-based designs instead describe
ranges of contiguous storage. Each representation encodes assumptions
about allocation, fragmentation, and common workloads.

The buffer cache keeps recently used blocks in memory and coordinates
access to blocks already being read or modified. A cache entry is not
just a bag of bytes. It has identity, state, ownership rules, and a
relationship to outstanding I/O. Two callers must not independently
modify unrelated cached copies of what is supposed to be one authoritative
block.

\section{Locality is a policy decision}

FFS improved performance by recognizing that the filesystem's allocation
policy affects the physical work required from the disk. Nearby metadata
and file blocks can reduce seeks; larger transfers can make better use
of bandwidth. Fragments reduce the internal waste that larger blocks
would otherwise impose on small files \parencite{ffs}.

Those choices reveal a limit of purely interface-oriented reasoning.
Two filesystems can present the same read and write calls while differing
dramatically in latency and throughput. An abstraction hides physical
layout from applications; it does not make physical layout irrelevant
to the implementation.

Solid-state storage changes the cost model. There is no mechanical seek,
but erase blocks, write amplification, controller behavior, queue depth,
and durability guarantees still matter. A policy optimized for one device
can become unnecessary or counterproductive on another. The enduring
Unix interface is compatible with repeated changes to the assumptions
beneath it.

\section{A successful write is not the whole durability story}
\index{fsync}\index{journaling}\index{crash recovery}

A program may write data that remains in a kernel cache.
The call's success can mean that the kernel accepted the bytes, not that
all relevant storage has survived a sudden power loss. Buffered libraries
add another layer: flushing a user-space buffer is different from requesting
that the kernel synchronize data to durable storage.

A file operation often modifies several blocks. Creating a file can
require an inode allocation, directory entry, allocation bitmap or free
list, and timestamps. If a crash preserves only some modifications,
the filesystem can contain contradictory claims about ownership.
A checker can repair certain inconsistencies after restart, but repair
may lose information or require expensive scanning.

Journaling records enough intent or changed state to make recovery
controlled. Copy-on-write filesystems use a different family of techniques,
writing new structures before switching references. Neither label alone
specifies every application-level durability guarantee. Metadata
consistency, data persistence, and the atomicity of a multi-file application
update are different properties.

The supplied xv6 filesystem uses a simple physical redo log.
Changed blocks are recorded in the log; a committed header marks a
transaction as recoverable; installation copies logged blocks to their
home locations. Recovery replays a committed transaction after restart.
Large writes are split to fit the log, so one application write is not
necessarily one indivisible persistent transaction
\parencite[sec. 7.5--7.6]{xv6}.

This is an excellent teaching mechanism because the commit point is
visible. It is not evidence that hardware can never tear a write or reorder
I/O. Real systems must match their protocol to the storage contract,
including flushes, barriers, checksums, or other mechanisms as required.
A recovery proof depends on what the device promises to preserve.

\section{Replacing a file safely takes more than rename}

Applications often write a new temporary file and rename it into place.
Within the specified same-filesystem conditions, rename can provide an
atomic namespace replacement to concurrent readers. That is a valuable
property: readers need not observe a half-written new file through the
final name.

Crash durability is a separate question. Depending on the filesystem and
platform, an application may need to synchronize the temporary file,
perform the rename, and synchronize the containing directory to preserve
the intended result across a crash. Cross-filesystem moves and updates
involving several names require different reasoning. The correct sequence
must be checked against the target system's guarantees.

This distinction between visibility and persistence is the filesystem's
version of a broader theme. A clean interface can make ordinary operation
simple while leaving exceptional guarantees explicit. Unix made files
easy to manipulate; reliable storage engineering still requires asking
what happens when the machine stops between two steps.
